caymanslm: expose the live SELinux policy sequence for selinux_hide

Duck Detector compares the /sys/fs/selinux/status page (sequence, policyload)
against the seqno returned in an av_decision from selinux_check_access. On
this kernel KSU advances status->sequence via reset_avc_cache while the real
policyload and the decision seqno stay at latest_granting, so an app sees a
"seqno split". avd_init() stamps av_decision.seqno = latest_granting, so export
latest_granting and let selinux_hide make the APP-FACING fake status page report
policyload = latest_granting and sequence = 2*latest_granting -- a consistent
pair that matches the decision seqno. Real page and policy are untouched.

Sorts after caymanslm-zzz-selinux-hide-injected-types.patch (both edit
services.c); adds only a new function, no hunk overlap.

diff --git a/security/selinux/ss/services.c b/security/selinux/ss/services.c
--- a/security/selinux/ss/services.c
+++ b/security/selinux/ss/services.c
@@ -2297,3 +2297,14 @@ bool selinux_sid_is_hidden_from_caller(u32 sid)
 #endif
 
+#ifdef CONFIG_KSU_SUSFS
+/* caymanslm: expose the live policy sequence (latest_granting) -- the same
+ * value avd_init() stamps into av_decision.seqno that an app reads via
+ * selinux_check_access. selinux_hide uses it to make the app-facing fake
+ * /sys/fs/selinux/status policyload/sequence match that seqno (no split). */
+u32 ksu_selinux_policy_seqno(void)
+{
+	return latest_granting;
+}
+#endif
+
 size_t security_policydb_len(void)
